{"text":"Caching frequently requested data can reduce the time an application spends waiting for a database. This can improve response times when the same information is needed repeatedly. It does not necessarily help requests that mostly access new data, and storing additional copies uses memory.

The main difficulty is deciding when a cached value is no longer valid. If updates are not reflected promptly, users may see outdated information even though the database contains the correct value. A short expiry time limits how long this can happen, but it also increases the number of database requests.

Teams should measure performance under realistic workloads before choosing a caching strategy. The appropriate approach depends on how often data changes and how much temporary inconsistency the application can tolerate. Monitoring should cover both speed and correctness, since faster responses are not useful if they return the wrong information.","context":{},"editing_guides":[]}
